Raise the echo estimate while the echo's delay has jumped with nothing marked

When the delay between what is played and what comes back in the microphone jumps with
nothing marked (no change of devices, no restart or move of a stream: a device's glitch,
the sound server's own buffering), AEC3 keeps its render buffer aligned to the old delay
until its delay estimator reports the new one, and the echo leaks through meanwhile: the
linear filter, and the residual echo estimate built on it, look at render blocks the echo
no longer comes from.

This patch moves no delay and restarts nothing. The matched filters' lag aggregator keeps
its last 16 lags and the delay it last reported; once the estimate is settled (a
significant candidate, 5 s since a hard reset) and the 16 lags have left the reported
delay (none within a block of it, at least 12 within a block of their median, the median
more than 8 blocks away), it sees a jump (MatchedFilterLagAggregator::EchoJumpLag,
EchoPathDelayEstimator::EchoJumpBlocks, RenderDelayController::EchoJumpBlocks). The block
processor hands the jump in blocks to the echo remover (EchoRemover::SetEchoJump) before
each capture block. While a jump is seen, the neural residual echo estimator is not
active and the render signal has had no narrow peak for 5 s, the echo remover keeps the
refined filters' frequency responses as they were at the jump's first block and raises
the linear echo estimate and the residual echo estimates (S2_linear, R2, R2_unbounded) to
at least that filter applied to the render spectra the jump points to, J - 1, J and J + 1
blocks away (the largest of the three), so the suppressor holds the leaked echo down
until the delay estimator realigns. Each jump is logged once ("AEC3: echo delay jumped N
blocks with nothing marked"). Everything else is as before.

In a simulation of an unmarked jump between 60 and 180 ms (384 jumps), the echo from
0.4 to 1.3 s after the jump went from -13.2 to -23.6 dB, and a word said 0.5 s after it
lost 0.04 dB (1.5 dB at the worst).

Measured: not yet.
